WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.500 --> 00:00:03.780
Command-line arguments can be more visible than teams expect.

2
00:00:03.880 --> 00:00:13.320
They may appear in shell history, job logs, process listings, debugging output, crash reports, copied support evidence, or CI/CD traces.

3
00:00:13.420 --> 00:00:24.220
That visibility makes arguments a poor place for secrets such as passwords, tokens, private keys, or sensitive connection strings when safer delivery patterns are available.

4
00:00:24.320 --> 00:00:33.440
Arguments may appear in shell history, job logs, process listings, debugging output, crash reports, copied support evidence, or CI/CD traces.

5
00:00:33.720 --> 00:00:43.960
When a script needs sensitive values, prefer approved secret-management mechanisms, controlled runtime injection, protected files, or platform identity where practical.

6
00:00:44.060 --> 00:00:49.900
The goal is to keep secrets out of command lines, logs, and long-lived diagnostic records.

7
00:00:50.000 --> 00:00:58.640
If a value must be supplied by an operator or platform, design the interface so the secret is not echoed back or stored in obvious places.

8
00:00:58.920 --> 00:01:00.680
Logging should be careful.

9
00:01:00.780 --> 00:01:08.220
Usage messages and error output should avoid dumping raw argument lists when those arguments may include sensitive values.

10
00:01:08.320 --> 00:01:11.760
Debug modes should redact sensitive option values.

11
00:01:11.860 --> 00:01:17.700
A message can identify that a required credential reference is missing without printing the credential.

12
00:01:17.800 --> 00:01:22.440
Logs should help operators understand behavior without creating a new exposure path.

13
00:01:22.720 --> 00:01:25.440
Treat argument logs as long-lived records.

14
00:01:25.540 --> 00:01:31.940
They may be searchable, exported, attached to tickets, or reviewed by people who did not run the script.

15
00:01:32.040 --> 00:01:39.560
Defensive argument handling includes deciding which values are safe to record, which should be summarized, and which should never appear in output.

16
00:01:39.660 --> 00:01:44.700
Safe logging preserves operational evidence while protecting sensitive data.
